WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:01.760
He's a recycling evangelist.

00:00:01.760 --> 00:00:03.360
The law did not agree.

00:00:03.360 --> 00:00:05.100
In a couple of days he's going to prison.

00:00:05.100 --> 00:00:07.600
There's no winning when you go up against
Microsoft.

00:00:07.600 --> 00:00:10.840
After a 6 year legal battle with the Department
of Justice,

00:00:10.840 --> 00:00:15.800
Eric Lundgren is reporting to a federal correctional
facility to begin a 15 month sentence.

00:00:15.800 --> 00:00:20.140
His crime was copying and selling CDs for
restoring the Windows operating system

00:00:20.140 --> 00:00:21.960
on broken PCs.

00:00:21.960 --> 00:00:25.140
So why did Lundgren's crime result in a jail
sentence?

00:00:25.140 --> 00:00:29.060
The answer cuts to the heart of a major battle
going on in the tech industry today.

00:00:29.060 --> 00:00:32.420
Companies are in a battle to preserve certain
aspects of US copyright law

00:00:32.420 --> 00:00:35.640
that give them enormous power over the products
we own

00:00:35.640 --> 00:00:38.980
and in the process they're trampling our First
Amendment rights.

00:00:42.820 --> 00:00:46.899
To better understand why Eric Lundgren posed
a major threat to the tech industry

00:00:46.899 --> 00:00:50.729
consider the third party repair shops that
have popped up all over the country

00:00:50.729 --> 00:00:54.960
allowing consumers to get their devices fixed
faster and cheaper.

00:00:59.360 --> 00:01:03.179
Or a company like IFIXIT, which runs an online
repair community

00:01:03.180 --> 00:01:07.040
where people post repair tutorials on a broad
range of products.

00:01:07.040 --> 00:01:08.860
Kyle Wiens is its co-founder.

00:01:08.860 --> 00:01:11.240
I like to say that if you can't fix it you
don't really own it.

00:01:11.240 --> 00:01:13.939
And there was this kinda dark period in the
80s and 90s

00:01:13.940 --> 00:01:16.360
where we had all of these new gizmos that
were coming out

00:01:16.360 --> 00:01:19.640
we have VCRs, we had camcorders, we had all
of these complex new gadgets

00:01:19.640 --> 00:01:21.440
and nobody really knew how to fix them

00:01:21.440 --> 00:01:25.080
and now that we have the internet all of this
information can get shared online.

00:01:25.080 --> 00:01:29.940
Wiens started the company in his dorm room
15 years ago when his first macbook broke.

00:01:29.940 --> 00:01:32.039
There had been information online on how to
fix it

00:01:32.040 --> 00:01:36.260
and they had gotten a legal takedown notice
from Apple and that made me angry

00:01:36.260 --> 00:01:40.799
and so I decided 'well if they don't want
people to know, maybe I want people to know.'

00:01:40.799 --> 00:01:45.040
And so went ahead, took it apart again, took
photos, put them online and the rest is history

00:01:45.040 --> 00:01:46.300
we've been doing it ever since.

00:01:46.300 --> 00:01:52.299
In 2016 IFIXIT did more than 21 million in
sales of tools and repair kits

00:01:52.300 --> 00:01:54.280
with 10 million monthly visitors,

00:01:54.280 --> 00:01:57.960
the company relies on its community of over 50 thousand contributors

00:01:57.960 --> 00:02:02.380
who offer repair tips that comprise a growing
library of open source manuals.

00:02:02.380 --> 00:02:07.820
We're going to Kansas, Pennsylvania, Minnesota,
we ship all over the world.

00:02:07.820 --> 00:02:11.820
First tech companies struck back by integrating
special screws and adhesives

00:02:11.820 --> 00:02:14.660
that aren't available from third party sellers.

00:02:14.660 --> 00:02:19.400
Then IFIXIT had it's developer account banned
and app removed from the Apple Store

00:02:19.400 --> 00:02:23.260
after it posted a tear down of the apple TV
in 2015.

00:02:23.260 --> 00:02:27.000
And for a period of time Apple was disabling
iPhone 6s

00:02:27.000 --> 00:02:31.120
when the company had detected work had been
done at independent repair shops.

00:02:31.120 --> 00:02:33.740
The company was later served with a class
action lawsuit

00:02:33.740 --> 00:02:36.720
and reimbursed thousands of effected consumers.

00:02:36.720 --> 00:02:41.480
But none of these tactics worked, IFIXIT didn't
need the app to operate their business

00:02:41.480 --> 00:02:45.120
and the company figured out a way around those
special screws and adhesives.

00:02:47.700 --> 00:02:51.120
So tech companies turned to a provision in
US copyright law

00:02:51.130 --> 00:02:55.930
Section 1201 of the Digital Millennium Copyright
Act, known as the DMCA

00:02:55.930 --> 00:02:59.500
which makes it illegal to break digital locks
protecting copyrighted work.

00:02:59.500 --> 00:03:01.900
The law is intent was to protect creative
content

00:03:01.900 --> 00:03:05.720
like movies, music and software from being
stolen and copied.

00:03:05.720 --> 00:03:10.640
And as software increasingly replaces hardware
underneath the hoods of our tech devices

00:03:10.640 --> 00:03:14.020
this appeal to copyright law had its desired
effect,

00:03:14.020 --> 00:03:17.080
shifting the balance of power over the stuff that you buy.

00:03:21.620 --> 00:03:25.800
Eric Lundgren was just a few weeks away from
going to prison the day we spoke with him.

00:03:25.800 --> 00:03:29.820
One man's trash is another man's treasure,
right?

00:03:29.820 --> 00:03:32.820
Well this is the world's trash and it's my
treasure.

00:03:32.820 --> 00:03:37.210
Before Lundgren landed in prison, he spent
his days running a company in Los Angeles

00:03:37.210 --> 00:03:42.430
that recycles about 40 million pounds of e-waste
annually.

00:03:42.430 --> 00:03:45.280
He's been passionate about the field since
he was a teenager.

00:03:45.280 --> 00:03:49.280
I grew up in a very small town, so I just
said 'well I'll help you recycle it.'

00:03:49.280 --> 00:03:54.920
By 18 I was doing it for American Airlines,
for Coca Cola, it just blew up very fast.

00:03:54.920 --> 00:03:58.860
All of this is, you know, keyboards and mice
and power cords

00:03:58.870 --> 00:04:03.340
and I mean a power cord, it's a cord, it can
be used again and again and again

00:04:03.340 --> 00:04:05.480
so if I get in truckloads of brand new power
cords,

00:04:05.480 --> 00:04:10.520
we're gonna put them with something new and
be able to use them.

00:04:10.520 --> 00:04:15.820
When he was 19, Lundgren relocated to Shenzhen,
a city in China's Guangdong province

00:04:15.820 --> 00:04:18.620
that became known as the Silicon Valley of
hardware.

00:04:18.620 --> 00:04:23.860
In 2011 he was basically working to source
generic parts from electronics

00:04:23.860 --> 00:04:27.380
and finding brokers in the United States and
basically working as a middleman there.

00:04:27.380 --> 00:04:30.900
That's Jake Swearingen, tech writer for New
York Magazine.

00:04:30.900 --> 00:04:35.720
A broker named Bob Wolff approached him, he
had already worked with Bob Wolff in the past.

00:04:35.720 --> 00:04:41.020
Bob Wolff would provide what's called a Dell
OEM, which is Original Equipment Manufacturer,

00:04:41.020 --> 00:04:43.280
a Dell OEM restore CD

00:04:43.280 --> 00:04:46.580
and Lundgren would take it to a duplicate
shop in china

00:04:46.580 --> 00:04:49.640
and get tens of thousands of copies made

00:04:49.640 --> 00:04:55.280
which then Bob Wolff would use as a broker
and sell to PC refurbishers here in the United States.

00:04:55.280 --> 00:05:00.000
When Lundgren took this Dell OEM restore CD
to the duplicate shop in China

00:05:00.000 --> 00:05:02.130
they didn't just duplicate the software on
there

00:05:02.130 --> 00:05:04.010
they duplicated everything about the CDs

00:05:04.010 --> 00:05:09.100
so that includes the labeling on it, so the
Dell logo, the Microsoft logo,

00:05:09.100 --> 00:05:12.600
everything about it  they just duplicated the whole thing wholesale.

00:05:12.600 --> 00:05:18.290
Then in 2012, one shipment got flagged by
customs, Wolff is approached by the feds

00:05:18.290 --> 00:05:21.180
participates in a sting operation targeting
Lundgren

00:05:21.180 --> 00:05:24.660
and then ultimately the feds raid Lundgren's
house in Florida.

00:05:24.660 --> 00:05:28.700
And both he and Wolff are eventually indicted
on about 21 counts

00:05:28.700 --> 00:05:33.980
that range from conspiracy to distribute counterfeit
goods to wire fraud

00:05:33.980 --> 00:05:35.660
and a whole host of other charges.

00:05:38.820 --> 00:05:41.460
So the government's case was that Lundgren
and Wolff

00:05:41.460 --> 00:05:46.300
were attempting to create a large scale counterfeiting
enterprise.

00:05:46.300 --> 00:05:49.740
Lundgren plead guilty to conspiracy to traffic
in counterfeit goods

00:05:49.740 --> 00:05:51.700
and criminal copyright infringement

00:05:51.700 --> 00:05:53.780
and there's no doubt that he's guilty of wrongdoing.

00:05:53.780 --> 00:05:55.290
Emails sent by Lundgren reveal

00:05:55.290 --> 00:05:59.580
that he even pressured Wolff to sell batches
of CDs that came out slightly defective

00:05:59.580 --> 00:06:03.960
showing his eagerness to make some money off
of this counterfeited merchandise.

00:06:03.960 --> 00:06:07.600
But what's less clear is the impact his actions
had on Microsoft.

00:06:07.600 --> 00:06:09.400
One of the real problems in this case

00:06:09.400 --> 00:06:12.900
that I feel like neither side was really able to fully resolve

00:06:12.900 --> 00:06:16.920
is that there is no real market for a Dell
OEM restore CD.

00:06:16.920 --> 00:06:22.380
And the reason for this is is that when you
buy a Dell, you get this restore CD for free.

00:06:22.380 --> 00:06:27.080
If you lose it at any point, you can download
the software off the internet for free.

00:06:27.080 --> 00:06:29.660
It doesn't look like he really hurt anyone
very seriously

00:06:29.660 --> 00:06:33.020
and yet they're going to basically put him
in a cage for 15 months.

00:06:33.020 --> 00:06:36.080
Tom W. Bell is a law professor at Chapman
University

00:06:36.080 --> 00:06:39.140
specializing in intellectual property and copyright.

00:06:39.140 --> 00:06:44.780
Microsoft may be firm on the law, but we can
question wether, you know,

00:06:44.780 --> 00:06:47.360
their motives are really about protecting
the software.

00:06:47.360 --> 00:06:51.300
Those motives are all about controlling software
through copyright law

00:06:51.300 --> 00:06:54.180
what tech companies are finding is that their
most powerful weapon

00:06:54.180 --> 00:06:57.400
against third party repair companies like
IFIXIT.

00:06:57.400 --> 00:07:01.160
Ever since 79 terms of copyright have gotten
longer,

00:07:01.160 --> 00:07:04.820
the rights protected have gotten more and
more broad,

00:07:04.820 --> 00:07:08.620
the sanctions against infringing have gotten
more and more punitive,

00:07:08.620 --> 00:07:11.400
all the founders cared about were books, maps
and charts,

00:07:11.400 --> 00:07:14.040
they knew about music and sculpture and paintings
and architecture,

00:07:14.040 --> 00:07:15.700
they didn't think it needed copyright.

00:07:15.700 --> 00:07:17.440
Enter the 20th century.

00:07:17.440 --> 00:07:20.840
For adults there's only one hot item: the
VCR.

00:07:20.840 --> 00:07:24.600
It's an invention that some say is changing
the whole idea of television.

00:07:24.600 --> 00:07:27.480
Movie studios said this violated the copyright
laws.

00:07:27.480 --> 00:07:30.220
What you started seeing was the music industry
and Hollywood

00:07:30.220 --> 00:07:33.840
really aggressively moving forward on the
idea that copyright infringement

00:07:33.840 --> 00:07:37.070
should become a criminal issue and not purely
a civil one

00:07:37.070 --> 00:07:42.280
and more particularly that people that were
infringing on a large scale basis serve time in jail.

00:07:42.280 --> 00:07:46.360
And copyright law was written so broadly,
it applied to software too.

00:07:46.360 --> 00:07:50.440
Copyright law doesn't distinguish, if you
have a lot of copyrights in music

00:07:50.440 --> 00:07:52.930
then you go and ask for stronger copyrights,

00:07:52.930 --> 00:07:54.930
many of the provisions you'd have change in
your favor

00:07:54.930 --> 00:07:58.780
like the term of copyright, gonna benefit
everybody who has a copyright.

00:07:58.780 --> 00:08:02.780
Then as the 2000s rolled along software was
suddenly everywhere.

00:08:02.780 --> 00:08:08.420
Now we are surrounded in copyright, we are
immersed in copyrighted content.

00:08:08.420 --> 00:08:12.940
So that means tractors and cars, it means
your smartphone,

00:08:12.940 --> 00:08:18.060
it means increasingly the refrigerators and
washing machines that people have in their homes.

00:08:18.060 --> 00:08:23.360
Kit Walsh is an attorney with the Electronic
Frontier Foundation, also known as EFF.

00:08:23.360 --> 00:08:27.180
She says that software's ubiquity has changed
the meaning of copyright law.

00:08:27.180 --> 00:08:32.300
There's a lingering hook that the seller has
in your property

00:08:32.300 --> 00:08:35.360
that they're arguing gives them really broad
powers

00:08:35.360 --> 00:08:39.420
to dictate how you use that property going forward.

00:08:39.420 --> 00:08:41.560
You're not even allowed to look at what's
in there,

00:08:41.560 --> 00:08:46.820
we sold you this but you're not allowed to
know how it actually works or what it's actually doing

00:08:46.820 --> 00:08:50.100
because that's our copyrighted code, you're
not allowed to look at it.

00:08:50.100 --> 00:08:54.400
And that's presented some unintended consequences
for security researchers.

00:08:54.400 --> 00:08:59.480
Software that's deployed in cars, tractors,
other important devices,

00:08:59.480 --> 00:09:03.080
has these controls on it, which is a good
thing for a security measure,

00:09:03.080 --> 00:09:10.760
the problem is when you the owner don't have
the keys or the permission to go in and open it up.

00:09:10.760 --> 00:09:15.780
So this interferes not just with repair but
also security research, safety research,

00:09:15.780 --> 00:09:18.600
like the folks who did crash test dummies.

00:09:18.600 --> 00:09:22.639
EFF is pushing for copyright reform on First
Amendment grounds.

00:09:22.640 --> 00:09:28.800
Code is a form of speech and courts have grappled
with that because code also feels a lot like a tool

00:09:28.800 --> 00:09:30.880
they say based on that functionality

00:09:30.880 --> 00:09:33.660
that it's not going to be as protected as other forms of speech

00:09:33.660 --> 00:09:37.220
even though we have case law that says if
you publish instructions,

00:09:37.220 --> 00:09:38.700
if you publish a cookbook,

00:09:38.700 --> 00:09:45.350
if you publish even instructions that are
dangerous to the reader then that's still speech.

00:09:45.350 --> 00:09:48.700
While EFF is challenging copyright protections
in court

00:09:48.700 --> 00:09:53.300
right to repair advocates are pushing for
legislation at the state and federal level.

00:09:53.300 --> 00:09:55.300
I was testifying in front of the copyright
office

00:09:55.300 --> 00:09:59.839
saying 'hey we need to ability to repair game
consoles in a cost effective manner.'

00:09:59.839 --> 00:10:02.519
and we had the Entertainment Software Association
opposing us

00:10:02.519 --> 00:10:06.939
saying 'no if people can repair their game
consoles it will enable rampant piracy.'

00:10:06.939 --> 00:10:10.329
So we're literally fighting an army of suits,
it feels like the Matrix sometimes

00:10:10.329 --> 00:10:15.260
where there's a new suit that appears from
around the corner every step of the way.

00:10:15.260 --> 00:10:18.360
We need to actually repeal sections of 1201
of the copyright act

00:10:18.360 --> 00:10:20.120
it's not doing anybody any good.

00:10:20.120 --> 00:10:23.060
And we also need to, these state level right
to repair laws

00:10:23.060 --> 00:10:25.040
where you have states across the country that
are saying

00:10:25.040 --> 00:10:28.600
'hey big manufacturers, if you're going to
sell products in our state

00:10:28.600 --> 00:10:31.670
you need to support our local repair industry,
you can't cut them out of the mix

00:10:31.670 --> 00:10:33.189
you can't monopolize service.'

00:10:33.189 --> 00:10:36.720
There are at least 18 states considering fair
repair bills.

00:10:36.720 --> 00:10:40.780
Which are modeled after the 2012 automotive
right to repair law in Massachusetts

00:10:40.780 --> 00:10:43.200
which lead to a national agreement with the
auto industry

00:10:43.200 --> 00:10:46.340
to allow independent repair shops to work on cars.

00:10:46.340 --> 00:10:50.400
What we want is competition in the marketplace,
we want the free market, we want-

00:10:50.410 --> 00:10:54.410
the independent repair shops shouldn't have
an advantage over the Apple store

00:10:54.410 --> 00:10:56.880
they should just be able to compete on an
equal footing.

00:10:56.880 --> 00:11:00.060
For me ownership is being able to understand
how something works,

00:11:00.060 --> 00:11:04.559
the ability to not be trapped in some ecosystem,
be dependent on somebody else

00:11:04.560 --> 00:11:09.840
I'd like to be able to be self reliant, to
be able to, if I have something go wrong,

00:11:09.840 --> 00:11:12.420
understand it enough to be able to fix it.

00:11:12.420 --> 00:11:14.900
Thats what Lundgren says he was trying to
do.

00:11:14.900 --> 00:11:19.079
When copyright is criminalized it's not just
making a criminal out of this Lundgren fellow

00:11:19.080 --> 00:11:23.200
but you can very plausibly sit down and examine
what we do every day,

00:11:23.200 --> 00:11:29.160
forwarding emails, posting stuff on Facebook,
watching an unauthorized copy of a movie on YouTube

00:11:29.160 --> 00:11:33.440
that all of us are probably many times a day
now violating copyright law

00:11:33.449 --> 00:11:36.600
and probably some of us, you know, should
worry about criminal law

00:11:36.600 --> 00:11:38.200
who owns the products that you buy?

00:11:38.200 --> 00:11:39.040
You do.

00:11:39.040 --> 00:11:44.600
And you need to be able to repair those and
use those and give those away

00:11:44.600 --> 00:11:48.620
and let other people benefit from whatever
it is that you rightfully own.

00:11:48.620 --> 00:11:53.520
If I have to go to prison and it's going to
raise awareness, I'm happy to go.

00:11:53.529 --> 00:11:58.550
I just want to be able to get people to become
aware

00:11:58.550 --> 00:12:02.040
and to be able to have the right to repair
what they legally own.

